[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dns_server
diagnosis_text:
The issue is with the DNS subsystem on the dns_server (BIND/named) and how clients rely on it. Internally scoped names (e.g., web0.local) resolve instantly, but any name outside the “local” zone or short, bare hostnames (e.g., “web0”) are refused. Evidence:
- The DNS server is running BIND and listening on 10.200.0.2:53 (netstat shows TCP/UDP 53).
- Internal FQDNs are authoritative and fast (curl http://web0.local from host_1_1_1_1 shows ~1 ms DNS).
- Queries for external names and shortnames are refused (dig google.com and dig web0 @10.200.0.2 both return status: REFUSED).
- Server configuration explicitly disables recursion (in /etc/bind/named.conf: “recursion no;”). The zone “local” is defined in /etc/bind/db.local with web0–web3 records, which works.
- Clients have no search domain (hosts’ /etc/resolv.conf only has “nameserver 10.200.0.2”), so bare names aren’t expanded to “.local”.

Mechanism: Browsers load internal pages quickly when records are cached or fully qualified, but stall unpredictably when the page or user triggers lookups for non-local or short names; those queries are refused immediately or lead to retries/fallbacks, appearing as intermittent “spinning.”

Root cause: The DNS server is configured as authoritative-only (recursion disabled), and clients lack a search suffix.

Corrective fix: On dns_server, enable recursion for internal clients and optionally add forwarders:
- In /etc/bind/named.conf (or named.conf.options), set “recursion yes;” and “allow-recursion { 10.0.0.0/8; };” and add forwarders if upstream resolvers exist.
- Reload named. Also, distribute a search suffix (“search local”) to clients (via DHCP or resolv.conf) so shortnames resolve consistently. 
=== END ===